---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
---

<GuideLayout slug="combine-music-library">
  <div class="gx-guide">
    <div class="gx-hero">
      <p class="gx-hero__kicker">Three platforms. One library.</p>
      <p class="gx-hero__line">
        Keep Spotify, SoundCloud, and YouTube together in one browser library. <strong>Paste what you
        already use</strong>, keep unmatched tracks visible, and play without installing another desktop
        client.
      </p>
      <div class="gx-glance">
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">♫</span> Spotify · SoundCloud · YouTube</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">0</span> Accounts needed</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">⬇</span> Nothing to install</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">↔</span> Copy or export</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-intro">
      A song should not disappear just because the link came from a different platform. <a href="https://music.maxxtopia.com">Playlistmaxxing</a>
      gives Spotify, SoundCloud, and YouTube one browser-based library for searching, sorting, shuffling,
      building playlists, and keeping playback close while you browse.
    </p>

    <div class="gx-callout">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">♫ The short answer</p>
      <p>
        Open Playlistmaxxing, paste a track, playlist, album, or set, then review any unresolved matches
        instead of assuming the import worked. Once the library is in place, use search, playlists, shuffle,
        the pop-out player, and synced lyrics from the same browser page. No account or install is required.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>✅ What you get in one library</h2>
    <ul class="gx-checks">
      <li><strong>Three sources:</strong> keep Spotify, SoundCloud, and YouTube items together instead of switching tabs.</li>
      <li><strong>Useful organization:</strong> search, sort, shuffle, and build playlists around the music you already have.</li>
      <li><strong>Playback that stays nearby:</strong> pop out the floating player, scrub, pause, skip, and adjust volume while you browse.</li>
      <li><strong>Discovery from your taste:</strong> use artists already in your library as recommendation seeds, then hide or mute what does not fit.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🛠 Build the library step by step</h2>
    <div class="gx-steps">
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">1</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Open Playlistmaxxing</h3>
          <p>Go to <a href="https://music.maxxtopia.com">music.maxxtopia.com</a>. It runs in the browser, so there is no desktop installer and no new account to create before you can start organizing music.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">2</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Paste what you already use</h3>
          <p>Add a track, playlist, album, or set from Spotify, SoundCloud, or YouTube. Start with one small playlist if you want to verify the matches before bringing in a larger library.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">3</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Review Transfer issues</h3>
          <p>Spotify imports look for full-song matches. If something cannot be matched automatically, it stays visible with its original link and a fix path. That is better than silently dropping half a playlist.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">4</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Organize and play</h3>
          <p>Search the combined library, sort it, make playlists, shuffle, or open the floating player. Synced lyrics follow the song in the same window, with colors pulled from the cover art.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">5</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Move or back up the library</h3>
          <p>Use <strong>Copy code</strong>, <strong>Paste code</strong>, or export a file when you want to move the library to another device or keep a backup. The library stays on your device until you choose to move it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-specs">
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">3</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Music sources</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">0</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Accounts needed</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">0</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Installs</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">Free</div><div class="gx-spec__l">To use</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>🚫 What it does not pretend to do</h2>
    <ul class="gx-nope">
      <li><strong>It does not guarantee every match:</strong> some platform links need a manual fix, so unresolved items stay visible.</li>
      <li><strong>It does not replace your source accounts:</strong> Playlistmaxxing is a browser library, not a new Spotify, SoundCloud, or YouTube subscription.</li>
      <li><strong>It does not make content permanent:</strong> playback still depends on the source being available.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="gx-callout gx-callout--cyan">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">🧭 The cleanest first test</p>
      <ul class="gx-mini">
        <li><strong>Paste one playlist</strong> from the platform you use most.</li>
        <li><strong>Fix one unresolved item</strong> so you understand the recovery path.</li>
        <li><strong>Copy or export the result</strong> before building a larger library.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-outro">
      <strong>Bottom line:</strong> Playlistmaxxing is for people who are tired of treating three music
      platforms like three separate libraries. Paste the links you already have, keep the exceptions visible,
      and use one browser page for the library and playback.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
